# `operators::wirtinger`

The Wirtinger derivative `∂/∂z` of a function of a complex variable.

## Signature

```cpp
operators::Operator operators::wirtinger(std::string_view variable, std::int64_t order = 1, operators::DiffOptions options = {});
```

## What It Does

A function of a complex variable `z = x + iy` can depend on `z` and on its conjugate `z̄` separately. The Wirtinger derivatives are `∂/∂z = ½(∂/∂x − i ∂/∂y)` and `∂/∂z̄ = ½(∂/∂x + i ∂/∂y)`. `wirtinger("z")` is the first; [`conjugate_wirtinger`](conjugate_wirtinger.md) is the second.

For a holomorphic function (one that depends on `z` only) `∂/∂z` is the ordinary complex derivative and `∂/∂z̄` is zero; that second condition is the Cauchy–Riemann equations written as one equation. For a function that also depends on `z̄`, such as `|z|²`, both derivatives are non-zero and together they carry all of the real information.

The rules for `conj`, `real`, `imag`, `abs` and `angle` are built in, so expressions that mix them differentiate exactly. Fractional powers, `log` and `sqrt` use the principal branch. Higher orders repeat the derivative: `wirtinger("z", 2)` is `∂²/∂z²`. For a function of a *real* parameter, whose values happen to be complex, use [`differential`](differential.md).

## Parameters

`std::string_view variable`
: The complex variable. It must be a variable of the function the operator is applied to.

`std::int64_t order`
: How many times to differentiate. Positive; default 1.

`operators::DiffOptions options`
: Non-smooth policy, fallback and size limit; see `DiffOptions`.

## Returns

An `Operator` whose application returns `∂ⁿf/∂zⁿ` as a `Function` with the same variables.

## Errors

- A non-positive order is refused.
- A variable the function lacks is refused when applied.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A holomorphic function has `∂/∂z` equal to the ordinary derivative and `∂/∂z̄` zero. For `z²` at `z = 1 + 2i`, `∂/∂z = 2z = 2 + 4i`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <complex>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"z"}, [](math::Function z) { return z * z; });
  const std::complex<double> point{1.0, 2.0};
  const std::complex<double> dz = operators::wirtinger("z")(f)(point).item<std::complex<double>>();
  const std::complex<double> dzbar = operators::conjugate_wirtinger("z")(f)(point).item<std::complex<double>>();
  out.writeln("d/dz  z^2 =", dz.real(), "+", dz.imag(), "i  (2z = 2 + 4i)");
  out.writeln("|d/dzb z^2| =", std::abs(dzbar));
}
```

### Example 2

A function of `z` and `z̄`: `z z̄ = |z|²`. Then `∂/∂z = z̄` and `∂/∂z̄ = z`. At `z = 1 + 2i` those are `1 − 2i` and `1 + 2i`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <complex>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"z"}, [](math::Function z) { return z * z.conj(); });
  const std::complex<double> point{1.0, 2.0};
  const std::complex<double> dz = operators::wirtinger("z")(f)(point).item<std::complex<double>>();
  const std::complex<double> dzbar = operators::conjugate_wirtinger("z")(f)(point).item<std::complex<double>>();
  out.writeln("d/dz  |z|^2 =", dz.real(), dz.imag(), "  (conj z = 1 - 2i)");
  out.writeln("d/dzb |z|^2 =", dzbar.real(), dzbar.imag(), "  (z = 1 + 2i)");
}
```

### Example 3

The built-in rules for the parts of a complex number. At `z = 3 + 4i`: `∂|z|/∂z = z̄/(2|z|) = (3 − 4i)/10`, and `∂(Re z)/∂z = ½`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <complex>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function modulus = math::function({"z"}, [](math::Function z) { return z.abs(); });
  const math::Function real_part = math::function({"z"}, [](math::Function z) { return z.real(); });
  const std::complex<double> point{3.0, 4.0};
  const std::complex<double> a = operators::wirtinger("z")(modulus)(point).item<std::complex<double>>();
  const std::complex<double> b = operators::wirtinger("z")(real_part)(point).item<std::complex<double>>();
  out.writeln("d|z|/dz  =", a.real(), a.imag(), "  (0.3 - 0.4i)");
  out.writeln("dRe/dz   =", b.real(), b.imag(), "  (0.5)");
}
```

### Example 4

Higher orders. `∂²/∂z²` of `z³` is `6z`, which is `6 + 12i` at `1 + 2i`. The order must be positive.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <complex>
#include <string>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto first_line = [](const pluss::Error &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    return text.substr(0, text.find('\n'));
  };

  const math::Function f = math::function({"z"}, [](math::Function z) { return z * z * z; });
  const std::complex<double> w = operators::wirtinger("z", 2)(f)(std::complex<double>{1.0, 2.0}).item<std::complex<double>>();
  out.writeln("d2/dz2 z^3 =", w.real(), w.imag());
  try { (void)operators::wirtinger("z", 0); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("order zero:", first_line(e)); }
}
```

